hawking-limit-ebd149c6.interpretation:
The formula describes the evaporation time (
 t_{evap}
 ) of a black hole. The proportionality is shown to be with the cube of its mass (
 M
 ), scaled by the solar mass (
 M_\odot
 ). The constant of proportionality is approximately 
 10^{67} 
 years. This means that a black hole with the mass of the Sun (
 M = M_\odot
 ) would take roughly 
 10^{67} 
 years to evaporate. Stellar-mass black holes (
 \text{around } 10 M_\odot
 ) would take 
 1000 
 times longer, or 
 10^{70} 
 years. Supermassive black holes, with masses millions or billions of times that of the Sun, would take an unimaginably longer time to evaporate, far exceeding the current age of the universe. The inverse relationship between mass and evaporation rate stems from the fact that larger black holes have lower surface temperatures and thus radiate less intensely.

hawking-limit-ebd149c6.limits_and_boundary:
As the mass of the black hole (
 M
 ) approaches zero, the evaporation time (
 t_{evap}
 ) approaches zero, indicating that infinitesimally small black holes would evaporate almost instantaneously. Conversely, as the mass of the black hole approaches infinity, the evaporation time (
 t_{evap}
 ) approaches infinity, meaning that extremely massive black holes would take an exceedingly long time to evaporate, essentially remaining stable over cosmological timescales. For practical astrophysical black holes, the evaporation time is vastly greater than the age of the universe.

probabilistic-2f24c297.conceptual_definition:
This formula, central to quantum mechanics, quantifies the probability of finding a quantum system in a specific state (
$a_n$
) when its overall state is described by a wavefunction (
$\psi$
). It arises from the Born rule, which interprets the square of the amplitude of a wavefunction component as a probability density.
